1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Welcome to Topic 5.

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:07,850
1, IPv4 and IPv6 for Servers, the first
topic of Learning Plan 5 on addressing.

3
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:11,300
You have typed IP addresses since Lab 1.

4
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:13,950
4; now you will understand them.

5
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:19,800
You will review the four IPv4 settings
every server needs and why servers are

6
00:00:20,050 --> 00:00:21,250
static.

7
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:26,300
You will spot private ranges and the APIPA
address that signals trouble.

8
00:00:26,550 --> 00:00:30,550
You will work out a subnet and split a
network.

9
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:36,000
Then you meet IPv6: how to read, shorten,
classify, set and test it.

10
00:00:36,250 --> 00:00:38,650
Every server needs four IPv4 settings.

11
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:40,900
Here they are for SRV01.

12
00:00:41,150 --> 00:00:42,750
The IP address, 192.

13
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:44,200
168.

14
00:00:44,450 --> 00:00:45,650
50.

15
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:48,300
20, identifies it on the network.

16
00:00:48,550 --> 00:00:50,150
The subnet mask, 255.

17
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:51,600
255.

18
00:00:51,850 --> 00:00:53,050
255.

19
00:00:53,300 --> 00:01:00,100
0, or slash 24, says which part of the
address is the network and which part is

20
00:01:00,350 --> 00:01:01,550
the host.

21
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:08,200
The default gateway is the router to other
networks; our pod has no router, so it

22
00:01:08,450 --> 00:01:09,650
stays empty.

23
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:11,900
And the DNS server, 192.

24
00:01:12,150 --> 00:01:13,350
168.

25
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:14,800
50.

26
00:01:15,050 --> 00:01:16,650
10, which is DC01.

27
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:18,900
SRV01 sends anything inside 192.

28
00:01:19,150 --> 00:01:20,350
168.

29
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:21,800
50.

30
00:01:22,050 --> 00:01:23,250
0 directly.

31
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:26,300
Anything else would go to the gateway.

32
00:01:26,550 --> 00:01:28,150
Why are servers static?

33
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:32,400
Because DNS records, firewall rules and
clients point at them.

34
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:36,650
If a server's address changed, all of that
would break.

35
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:41,300
Next, the private ranges from RFC 1918: 10
slash 8, 172.

36
00:01:41,550 --> 00:01:43,550
16 slash 12, and 192.

37
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:45,000
168 slash 16.

38
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:50,450
Anyone can use them inside an
organization, and they are never routed on

39
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:51,900
the internet.

40
00:01:52,150 --> 00:01:53,350
Finally, APIPA.

41
00:01:53,600 --> 00:02:00,000
When a computer set to automatic gets no
answer from DHCP, it gives itself a 169.

42
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:01,450
254 address.

43
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:04,900
On a server, the static address is
missing.

44
00:02:05,150 --> 00:02:08,350
Let's refresh subnetting with our own pod
network.

45
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:15,800
Slash 24 means 24 ones in the mask, so the
first three octets are the network and the

46
00:02:16,050 --> 00:02:19,250
last octet, 8 bits, is the host part.

47
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:23,100
All host bits zero gives the network
address, 192.

48
00:02:23,350 --> 00:02:24,550
168.

49
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:26,000
50.

50
00:02:26,250 --> 00:02:27,450
0.

51
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:30,100
All ones gives the broadcast, 192.

52
00:02:30,350 --> 00:02:31,550
168.

53
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:33,000
50.

54
00:02:33,250 --> 00:02:34,450
255.

55
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:35,900
Everything between, .

56
00:02:36,150 --> 00:02:37,350
1 to .

57
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:38,800
254, is usable.

58
00:02:39,050 --> 00:02:45,050
That is 2 to the power 8, which is 256,
minus 2, so 254 hosts.

59
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:48,900
Our servers, CLIENT01 and the DHCP scope
all fit.

60
00:02:49,150 --> 00:02:50,750
A campus owns 10.

61
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:52,200
50.

62
00:02:52,450 --> 00:02:53,650
0.

63
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:57,900
0 slash 16 and wants four subnets, one per
building.

64
00:02:58,150 --> 00:03:02,150
Four is 2 times 2, so borrow 2 host bits.

65
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:07,200
The prefix grows from 16 to 18, and the
mask becomes 255.

66
00:03:07,450 --> 00:03:08,650
255.

67
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:10,100
192.

68
00:03:10,350 --> 00:03:11,550
0.

69
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:19,400
The block size is 256 minus 192, which is
64, so subnets start at 0, 64, 128 and 192

70
00:03:19,650 --> 00:03:21,250
in the third octet.

71
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:24,700
Each has 14 host bits, so 16,382 hosts.

72
00:03:24,950 --> 00:03:26,150
Check: 10.

73
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:27,600
50.

74
00:03:27,850 --> 00:03:29,050
130.

75
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:33,700
7 sits between 128 and 191, so it is in
10.

76
00:03:33,950 --> 00:03:35,150
50.

77
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:36,600
128.

78
00:03:36,850 --> 00:03:38,050
0 slash 18.

79
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:41,900
IPv6 addresses are 128 bits long, four
times IPv4.

80
00:03:42,150 --> 00:03:48,550
They are eight groups of four hexadecimal
digits, zero to nine and a to f, separated

81
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:50,000
by colons.

82
00:03:50,250 --> 00:03:56,650
A subnet is almost always slash 64: the
first 64 bits are the network prefix, and

83
00:03:56,900 --> 00:04:01,300
the last 64 bits are the interface ID, the
host part.

84
00:04:01,550 --> 00:04:03,150
IPv4 has about 4.

85
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:06,600
3 billion addresses, and the world ran
out.

86
00:04:06,850 --> 00:04:11,250
IPv6 gives every single subnet 2 to the
power 64 addresses.

87
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:14,300
Our pod prefix is fd00:50:: slash 64.

88
00:04:14,550 --> 00:04:16,550
There are two shortening rules.

89
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:20,400
Rule one: drop the leading zeros in each
group.

90
00:04:20,650 --> 00:04:24,250
0050 becomes 50, and 0000 becomes a single
0.

91
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:28,100
Zeros at the end stay, so fd00 stays fd00.

92
00:04:28,350 --> 00:04:33,150
Rule two: replace one run of all-zero
groups with a double colon.

93
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:38,200
Only once, or nobody could tell how many
zeros each one hides.

94
00:04:38,450 --> 00:04:40,850
Our long SRV01 address becomes
fd00:50::20.

95
00:04:41,100 --> 00:04:47,100
To expand, work backwards: fill each group
to four digits, then add zero groups until

96
00:04:47,350 --> 00:04:48,550
there are eight.

97
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:52,000
You will meet five kinds of IPv6 address.

98
00:04:52,250 --> 00:04:53,850
Link-local starts with fe80.

99
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:59,300
Windows makes one on every adapter; it
works only on the local link.

100
00:04:59,550 --> 00:05:05,150
Unique local addresses, fc00 slash 7, with
fd00 actually in use, are the private

101
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:07,000
ones, like RFC 1918.

102
00:05:07,250 --> 00:05:10,050
Global unicast, 2000 slash 3, are public.

103
00:05:10,300 --> 00:05:13,500
The loopback is colon colon 1, like 127.

104
00:05:13,750 --> 00:05:14,950
0.

105
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:16,400
0.

106
00:05:16,650 --> 00:05:17,850
1.

107
00:05:18,100 --> 00:05:21,700
And multicast starts with ff; IPv6 has no
broadcast.

108
00:05:21,950 --> 00:05:27,150
The percent number after a link-local
address is the zone ID, the adapter's

109
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:28,600
interface index.

110
00:05:28,850 --> 00:05:32,850
First, every host makes a link-local
address, with no help.

111
00:05:33,100 --> 00:05:37,900
Then a router sends a Router Advertisement
with the slash 64 prefix.

112
00:05:38,150 --> 00:05:40,950
The host adds its own interface ID.

113
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:45,200
That is SLAAC, stateless address
autoconfiguration, and no server is

114
00:05:45,450 --> 00:05:46,650
needed.

115
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:51,700
It checks that nobody else uses the
address, then starts using it.

116
00:05:51,950 --> 00:05:57,550
Flags in the advertisement can also send
hosts to DHCPv6, either for the address

117
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:00,600
itself or just for options like DNS.

118
00:06:00,850 --> 00:06:04,850
Our pod has no router, so you set static
addresses.

119
00:06:05,100 --> 00:06:08,300
Before you change anything, read what is
there.

120
00:06:08,550 --> 00:06:13,750
ipconfig slash all on SRV01 shows DHCP
Enabled: No, so IPv4 is static.

121
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,600
Next comes a link-local IPv6 address
starting with fe80.

122
00:06:17,850 --> 00:06:24,250
Windows made it, so yours is different,
and you never write it down as a setting.

123
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:30,500
Then the IPv4 address and mask you typed,
an empty default gateway, and the DNS

124
00:06:30,750 --> 00:06:31,950
server 192.

125
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:33,400
168.

126
00:06:33,650 --> 00:06:34,850
50.

127
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:36,300
10, DC01.

128
00:06:36,550 --> 00:06:38,150
Every member of sa1.

129
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:40,400
lab uses DC01 for DNS.

130
00:06:40,650 --> 00:06:44,250
Keep this output in mind: it is your
baseline.

131
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:46,500
Here is the GUI way.

132
00:06:46,750 --> 00:06:47,950
Open ncpa.

133
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:50,600
cpl, right-click Ethernet and choose
Properties.

134
00:06:50,850 --> 00:06:56,050
Select Internet Protocol Version 6, TCP
slash IPv6, and keep its box checked.

135
00:06:56,300 --> 00:06:57,500
Then select Properties.

136
00:06:57,750 --> 00:07:00,150
Choose Use the following IPv6 address.

137
00:07:00,400 --> 00:07:02,000
For DC01, type fd00:50::10.

138
00:07:02,250 --> 00:07:04,650
In Subnet prefix length, type 64.

139
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:08,500
That is a number of bits, not a mask.

140
00:07:08,750 --> 00:07:14,750
Leave Default gateway empty, because the
pod has no router, and leave the DNS boxes

141
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:16,200
as they are.

142
00:07:16,450 --> 00:07:20,050
Then select OK, and Close the Ethernet
Properties window.

143
00:07:20,300 --> 00:07:23,100
Every GUI task has a PowerShell twin.

144
00:07:23,350 --> 00:07:27,350
On a new server, find the adapter name
with Get-NetAdapter.

145
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:32,000
Then New-NetIPAddress with the interface
alias, the IP address and dash

146
00:07:32,250 --> 00:07:33,450
PrefixLength 24.

147
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:37,300
Then check with ipconfig that DHCP Enabled
shows No.

148
00:07:37,550 --> 00:07:40,750
With a router you would add dash
DefaultGateway.

149
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,400
Set-DnsClientServerAddress sets the DNS
server list.

150
00:07:43,650 --> 00:07:46,850
Finally, Get-NetIPConfiguration is the
PowerShell version of ipconfig.

151
00:07:47,100 --> 00:07:50,300
SRV01 already has these values from Lab 1.

152
00:07:50,550 --> 00:07:52,150
4 and Lab 4.

153
00:07:52,400 --> 00:07:56,000
3, so in the lab you only read them.

154
00:07:56,250 --> 00:07:59,450
For IPv6 you use the very same cmdlet.

155
00:07:59,700 --> 00:08:03,300
New-NetIPAddress, interface Ethernet, IP
address fd00:50::20, prefix length 64.

156
00:08:03,550 --> 00:08:07,150
Windows sees the colons and creates an
IPv6 address.

157
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:11,000
Then Get-NetIPAddress with dash
AddressFamily IPv6 lists the IPv6

158
00:08:11,250 --> 00:08:12,450
addresses.

159
00:08:12,700 --> 00:08:16,700
Your new one shows PrefixOrigin Manual,
because you typed it.

160
00:08:16,950 --> 00:08:21,350
The link-local one shows WellKnown; it
stays, and that is fine.

161
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:25,600
The loopback colon colon 1 sits on its own
pseudo-interface.

162
00:08:25,850 --> 00:08:29,850
Set-DnsClientServerAddress also accepts
IPv6 DNS server addresses, if a network

163
00:08:30,100 --> 00:08:31,300
ever needs them.

164
00:08:31,550 --> 00:08:32,750
Now test.

165
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:34,200
One catch first.

166
00:08:34,450 --> 00:08:35,650
In Lab 1.

167
00:08:35,900 --> 00:08:38,700
4 you opened ping for IPv4 only.

168
00:08:38,950 --> 00:08:44,950
IPv6 ping uses ICMPv6, and it has its own
firewall rule: File and Printer Sharing,

169
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:46,400
Echo Request, ICMPv6-In.

170
00:08:46,650 --> 00:08:50,250
Enable it on each computer you want to
ping.

171
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:52,900
Then ping dash 6 forces IPv6.

172
00:08:53,150 --> 00:08:57,150
Ping DC01 at fd00:50::10 and you should
get four replies.

173
00:08:57,400 --> 00:09:01,000
Test-Connection is the PowerShell ping;
here it reaches CLIENT01.

174
00:09:01,250 --> 00:09:07,250
Test by address in this lab, and you will
see why on the next slide.

175
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:12,700
When a name has both IPv4 and IPv6
addresses, Windows prefers IPv6 global

176
00:09:12,950 --> 00:09:14,150
addresses by default.

177
00:09:14,400 --> 00:09:18,000
A prefix policy table decides, and
Get-NetPrefixPolicy shows it.

178
00:09:18,250 --> 00:09:24,250
A detail: that table ranks unique local
addresses below IPv4, so in our pod names

179
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:26,500
may still answer on IPv4.

180
00:09:26,750 --> 00:09:28,350
Now the important rule.

181
00:09:28,600 --> 00:09:31,000
Microsoft does not recommend disabling
IPv6.

182
00:09:31,250 --> 00:09:36,850
Windows uses it internally, and on a
domain controller LDAP over UDP 389 stops

183
00:09:37,100 --> 00:09:38,300
working.

184
00:09:38,550 --> 00:09:42,950
If IPv4 really must win, set
DisabledComponents to hex 20 instead.

185
00:09:43,200 --> 00:09:44,800
Four mistakes to avoid.

186
00:09:45,050 --> 00:09:49,850
One: typing a dotted mask or slash 24 for
an IPv6 address.

187
00:09:50,100 --> 00:09:52,900
IPv6 uses a prefix length, normally 64.

188
00:09:53,150 --> 00:09:55,550
Two: using the double colon twice.

189
00:09:55,800 --> 00:09:59,800
Use it once, for the longest run of zero
groups.

190
00:10:00,050 --> 00:10:04,050
Three: clearing the IPv6 check box to keep
things simple.

191
00:10:04,300 --> 00:10:08,700
Keep IPv6 bound, and prefer IPv4 with hex
20 if needed.

192
00:10:08,950 --> 00:10:13,350
Four: ping dash 6 times out, so you retype
the address.

193
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:17,600
Check the firewall first, because the
ICMPv6 rule is separate.

194
00:10:17,850 --> 00:10:19,450
Time to check yourself.

195
00:10:19,700 --> 00:10:22,100
First, a file server shows 169.

196
00:10:22,350 --> 00:10:23,550
254.

197
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:25,000
20.

198
00:10:25,250 --> 00:10:26,450
7.

199
00:10:26,700 --> 00:10:30,700
What does that tell you, and what is the
fix?

200
00:10:30,950 --> 00:10:33,750
Second, how many hosts fit in 10.

201
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:35,200
50.

202
00:10:35,450 --> 00:10:36,650
64.

203
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:40,500
0 slash 18, and what is its broadcast
address?

204
00:10:40,750 --> 00:10:46,750
Third, shorten fd00:0050 followed by five
zero groups and 0020, and say what type of

205
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:48,200
address it is.

206
00:10:48,450 --> 00:10:54,050
Fourth, a colleague wants to clear IPv6 on
DC01 because we only use IPv4.

207
00:10:54,300 --> 00:10:55,900
What do you say?

208
00:10:56,150 --> 00:11:00,150
Pause the video, answer in your own words,
then continue.

209
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:02,000
Here are the answers.

210
00:11:02,250 --> 00:11:03,850
One: that is APIPA.

211
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:08,100
The adapter is on automatic and no DHCP
server answered.

212
00:11:08,350 --> 00:11:10,750
Give the server its static address.

213
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:17,000
Two: slash 18 leaves 14 host bits, so
16,382 hosts, and the broadcast is 10.

214
00:11:17,250 --> 00:11:18,450
50.

215
00:11:18,700 --> 00:11:19,900
127.

216
00:11:20,150 --> 00:11:23,350
255, just before the next subnet at 10.

217
00:11:23,600 --> 00:11:24,800
50.

218
00:11:25,050 --> 00:11:26,250
128.

219
00:11:26,500 --> 00:11:27,700
0.

220
00:11:27,950 --> 00:11:33,150
Three: fd00:50::20, a unique local
address, because fd00 slash 8 is the ULA

221
00:11:33,400 --> 00:11:34,600
range in use.

222
00:11:34,850 --> 00:11:36,450
Four: keep IPv6 enabled.

223
00:11:36,700 --> 00:11:41,900
Microsoft does not recommend disabling it,
and Active Directory on DC01 depends on

224
00:11:42,150 --> 00:11:43,350
it.

225
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:46,000
Let's do it in the pod.

226
00:11:46,250 --> 00:11:51,850
On SRV01, ipconfig slash all shows a
static IPv4 address and a link-local IPv6

227
00:11:52,100 --> 00:11:53,700
address that Windows made.

228
00:11:53,950 --> 00:11:59,550
On DC01, I open the TCP slash IPv6
properties, choose Use the following IPv6

229
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:03,800
address, type fd00:50::10 with prefix
length 64, and select OK.

230
00:12:04,050 --> 00:12:07,650
On SRV01, New-NetIPAddress adds
fd00:50::20, and Get-NetIPAddress shows it

231
00:12:07,900 --> 00:12:09,100
as Manual.

232
00:12:09,350 --> 00:12:14,550
With the ICMPv6 echo rule enabled and
CLIENT01 addressed, ping dash 6 gets

233
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:16,000
replies.

234
00:12:16,250 --> 00:12:19,450
Finally, Get-NetPrefixPolicy shows why we
test by address.

235
00:12:19,700 --> 00:12:26,500
To wrap up: a server needs an address, a
prefix, a gateway if there is a router,

236
00:12:26,750 --> 00:12:29,150
and DNS, and it is static.

237
00:12:29,400 --> 00:12:30,600
Read 169.

238
00:12:30,850 --> 00:12:32,850
254 as a DHCP problem.

239
00:12:33,100 --> 00:12:39,500
A subnet has a network address, a host
range and a broadcast, and you borrow bits

240
00:12:39,750 --> 00:12:40,950
to split it.

241
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:45,200
IPv6 is 128 bits, slash 64, shortened with
two rules.

242
00:12:45,450 --> 00:12:46,650
Keep it on.

243
00:12:46,900 --> 00:12:48,100
In Lab 5.

244
00:12:48,350 --> 00:12:53,950
1, about 60 minutes and practice only, you
read settings, subnet on paper, add

245
00:12:54,200 --> 00:12:57,400
fd00:50 addresses and test with ping dash
6.
